{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/DevOps Agent Memory Types.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/DevOps Agent Memory Types.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"dev ops agent memory types dev ops agent memory types","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/DevOps Agent Memory Types.md","fileName":"DevOps Agent Memory Types.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"DevOps Agent Memory Types","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 12:11\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[DevOps Agent Memory and MCP]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["DevOps Agent Memory and MCP","agenticaifordevopsengineers.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/DevOps Agent Memory Types.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/DevOps Agent Memory Types.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"dev ops agent memory types dev ops agent memory types","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/DevOps Agent Memory Types.md","fileName":"DevOps Agent Memory Types.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"DevOps Agent Memory Types","heading":"DevOps Agent Memory Types","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"DevOps agents use several kinds of memory. Short-term memory holds the current task and conversational state; long-term memory retains validated historical knowledge or policy; event-based memory records system events and outcomes; and workflow memory records which investigation steps have already occurred.\n\nThese categories serve different purposes and should not share one indiscriminate store. A temporary prompt context may be discarded, while a validated deployment convention can be durable. Making the category explicit helps define provenance, access, retention, and the risk of allowing that information to influence future decisions.","tags":["baby"],"links":["DevOps Agent Memory and MCP","agenticaifordevopsengineers.pdf"],"properties":[],"